          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/security-basics.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Security Basics\\ndescription: Fundamental security principles for safe system operation and defence against injection and traversal attacks.\\ntimestamp: 2025-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/security\\n  - concept/defensive-practices\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022One structured source; principles are well-established in security literature.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n# Security Basics\\n\\nAlways scope file writes to approved wiki paths.\\n\\n## Mitigation Checklist\\n\\nA layered defence against common attack patterns:\\n\\n1. **Principle of Least Privilege** \\u2014 Grant write scope only to the paths a process genuinely needs. Avoid blanket permissions; use explicit, narrow allowlists instead.\\n\\n2. **Input Validation at Trust Boundaries** \\u2014 Treat all external content as untrusted data, never as instructions. Distinguish between data and directives at every point where external content enters the system.\\n\\n3. **Path Canonicalization** \\u2014 Resolve and validate file paths before any write to block directory traversal. Reject paths that attempt to escape the intended scope (e.g., \\u0060../\\u0060 sequences).\\n\\n4. **Audit Logging** \\u2014 Record every denied action with its reason for later review. Logging enables post-incident analysis and helps identify attack patterns.\\n\u0022}"
